  "worked_solution": "First, we calculate the derivative of $r(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{\u2032}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}-2)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2})\\,j \\\\  & =2t\\,i+2t\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe vectors $r(t)$ and ${r}^{\u2032}(t)$ are perpendicular for some value of the parameter $t$ if and only if $r(t)\\cdot {r}^{\u2032}(t)=0.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t)\\cdot {r}^{\u2032}(t) & =0 \\\\ [({t}^{2}-2)\\,i+{t}^{2}\\,j]\\cdot (2t\\,i+2t\\,j) & =0 \\\\ ({t}^{2}-2)\\cdot 2t+{t}^{2}\\cdot 2t & =0 \\\\ 2t(2{t}^{2}-2) & =0 \\\\ 4t({t}^{2}-1) & =0 \\\\ 4t(t-1)(t+1) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $t\\in (0,\\infty )$, the only solution is $t=1.$\n\nFinally, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(1) & =((1)^{2}-2)\\,i+(1)^{2}\\,j=-\\,i+j,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n we conclude that $r(t)$ and ${r}^{\u2032}(t)$ are perpendicular at the point $(-1,1).$",
